---
// Hand-authored inline SVG: the identifier/credential split, and the one system
// where a single field does both jobs. Inline for the same reasons as
// ThreeQuestions.astro — see that file's header.
//
// Transcribed from the post, cell -> sentence:
//   Italy    "the codice fiscale is my username, the CIE is my password";
//            "it is printed on my identity card without being issued by it";
//            "an NFC chip that authenticates me directly. Since the physical
//             card has to be present, it satisfies the security requirement"
//   Portugal "Either way the credential stays separate from the document: most
//             commonly the Chave Móvel Digital, though the Cartão de Cidadão's
//             own certificates and an eIDAS credential from another member
//             state authenticate just as well"; the NIF is the username, per
//             "The NIF acts as a stable join key" in the ranking section
//   US       "The Social Security number is a username too, but it has to serve
//             as the password as well: a shared secret that never changes, is
//             the same at every institution, and is routinely accepted over the
//             phone"; "That's the actual flaw: one credential doing two jobs it
//             was never built for."
//             (Both quotes were re-synced in Aug 2026 — the versions previously
//             cited here had been edited out of the post and no longer matched.)
//   footnote eCBSV, launched 2020, legislated 2018 — https://www.ssa.gov/dataexchange/eCBSV/
//            "it answers only whether a name, a date of birth, and a number
//             match SSA's records. That confirms the number belongs to that
//             name. It does not confirm that the person in front of you *is*
//             that name."
//
// As in ThreeQuestions.astro, the US box SPANS both rows on purpose and the
// other two columns show a gap with an arrow across it. The gap is what makes
// the span legible; do not close it, and do not split the US box.
//
// The eCBSV note belongs to the password row and is deliberately phrased as
// what the service CANNOT do. It is the narrowest true statement available: a
// reader who takes "there is now a verification API" as the fix has read the
// diagram backwards, which is exactly the misreading the post spends a
// paragraph heading off.
//
// Styles live in global.css (.post-diagram, .dg-*) per the one-stylesheet rule.
// Marker and aria IDs are prefixed dg-cred-* — four diagrams share this page.
---

<figure class="post-figure post-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 700 290"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="dg-cred-title dg-cred-desc"
    class="dg"
  >
    <title id="dg-cred-title">
      Identifier and credential in three systems, and the one that merges them
    </title>
    <desc id="dg-cred-desc">
      Two rows, username and password, across three columns. Italy pairs the
      codice fiscale as the username with the CIE identity card as the password:
      two separate boxes, with an arrow from the first to the second. Portugal
      pairs the NIF with the Chave Móvel Digital the same way. The United States
      column has no pair — a single box spanning both rows holds the Social
      Security number, which serves as identifier and, because no second
      credential is universally held, as the proof of identity too. One field
      doing two jobs. A footnote records that the eCBSV service, launched in
      2020, confirms only that a number belongs to a name, not that the person
      presenting it is that name.
    </desc>

    <defs>
      <marker
        id="dg-cred-arrow"
        viewBox="0 0 10 10"
        refX="9"
        refY="5"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto-start-reverse"
      >
        <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" class="dg-arrowhead" />
      </marker>
    </defs>

    {/* ---- column headers ---- */}
    <text x="244" y="24" class="dg-call">Italy</text>
    <text x="426" y="24" class="dg-call">Portugal</text>
    <text x="608" y="24" class="dg-call">US citizen</text>

    <path class="dg-edge" d="M6 34 H 694" />

    {/* ---- row labels ---- */}
    <text x="6" y="85" class="dg-call dg-call--start">username</text>
    <text x="6" y="175" class="dg-call dg-call--start">password</text>

    {/* ---- the pairing arrows: two boxes, one edge between them ---- */}
    <g class="dg-edges" fill="none" marker-end="url(#dg-cred-arrow)">
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M244 112 V 134" />
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M426 112 V 134" />
    </g>

    {/* ---- Italy ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="158" y="48" width="172" height="62" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="244" y="76" class="dg-label">codice fiscale</text>
      <text x="244" y="95" class="dg-sub">not issued by any document</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="158" y="138" width="172" height="62" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="244" y="166" class="dg-label">CIE</text>
      <text x="244" y="185" class="dg-sub">NFC chip — card must be present</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- Portugal ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="340" y="48" width="172" height="62" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="426" y="76" class="dg-label">NIF</text>
      <text x="426" y="95" class="dg-sub">handed over at the register</text>
    </g>
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="340" y="138" width="172" height="62" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="426" y="166" class="dg-label">Chave Móvel Digital</text>
      <text x="426" y="185" class="dg-sub">or the CC's certificates, or eIDAS</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- the US: one box, no arrow, because there is nothing to point at ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="522" y="48" width="172" height="152" class="dg-node dg-node--anchor" />
      <text x="608" y="118" class="dg-label">SSN</text>
      <text x="608" y="138" class="dg-sub">identifier, then password</text>
    </g>

    <text x="608" y="224" class="dg-call">one field, two jobs</text>

    <text x="6" y="256" class="dg-note dg-note--start">
      Since 2020 a bank can ask eCBSV whether a name, a date of birth and a number match SSA's records.
    </text>
    <text x="6" y="272" class="dg-note dg-note--start">
      That confirms the number belongs to that name. Not that the person holding it is that name.
    </text>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    Read each column top to bottom: a public identifier, then the separate
    thing that proves you are the person it names. Italy pairs the codice
    fiscale with the CIE, Portugal the NIF with the Chave Móvel Digital. The US
    never issued the second half, so the Social Security number answers for
    both.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
